# WirePlumber Audio Routing Configuration
# Generated by ZepOS - DO NOT EDIT DIRECTLY!
# To customise: copy the template to $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zepos/templates/wireplumber-config.template
#
# NOTHING IN THIS FILE NAMES A DEVICE OF ITS OWN.
#
# The origin pinned two of the author's own microphones here by the ALSA
# node names their USB product strings produce - a headset and a webcam.
# A node name belongs to one machine's hardware, so on every other desk
# those rules matched nothing at all, and there was no way to see that:
# a WirePlumber rule whose match never fires is indistinguishable from
# one that fires. The file parses, the daemon starts, nothing is logged,
# and the routing the file describes simply does not happen.
#
# Everything below is built from the user settings instead. Blank is the
# shipping state and a perfectly good desktop - PipeWire then keeps its
# own choice of default device and connects what it always would.
#
#   zepos-settings set audio.default_sink   <node name>
#   zepos-settings set audio.default_source <node name>
#   zepos-settings set audio.blocked_sources '["<node name>", ...]'
#
# The fourth setting belongs to the file generated beside this one -
# ~/.config/easyeffects/db/easyeffectsrc - and is named here because that
# file cannot say so itself. It is KConfig, the format EasyEffects 8 uses
# since it became a Qt6/Kirigami application, and KConfig rewrites the
# whole file from its own entry map whenever the application saves.
# Measured against kconfig 6.27: a leading `#` comment is read without
# complaint and every key still resolves, and it is gone after the first
# save. A header there would break nothing - it would quietly disappear
# and leave a generated file looking hand-written.
#
#   zepos-settings set audio.effects_input  <node name>
#
# That one is the HARDWARE microphone the effects chain processes, which
# is not the same as audio.default_source: where the chain is the
# default, default_source names the virtual node the effects application
# publishes and effects_input names the device behind it.
#
# The node name of an attached device is the second column of
#   pactl list short sources
# and `wpctl status` shows the same devices with the descriptions a
# person recognises them by.
#
# Whether the names in the settings still match anything attached - after
# a cable moved, a firmware update renamed a device, or the settings were
# copied from another machine:
#
#   python3 {{ZEPOS_SYSTEM_ROOT}}/audio.py --check

# Default devices. Written only for the keys that carry a value: pinning
# a default to a node that is not there is not a harmless no-op, it is a
# default device nothing can play through - silence, with no error
# anywhere.
